# Byte-exact checkouts on every platform.
#
# Found by running qyro_crypto's test suite on Windows for the first time, in
# sprint 4C.1. Three tests failed there and passed everywhere else, and all
# three failed for the same reason: Git's default on Windows checks text files
# out with CRLF, so the bytes on disk were not the bytes that were committed.
#
# Two things in this repository depend on that not happening.
#
# The committed test vectors are the interoperability contract. `aead-v1.json`
# and `handshake-v1.json` are compared byte for byte against what the code
# regenerates, and an implementation in Swift or Kotlin is meant to hash exactly
# these files. A vector file that arrives with different bytes on Windows is a
# different file, and the whole point of committing it is that it is not.
#
# Several tests read this repository's own source to assert a structural
# property — that an ephemeral secret's exchange consumes `self`, that no
# `assert!` survives on the AEAD path. They match on strings containing `\n`.
#
# `eol=lf` rather than `text=auto` alone: `auto` normalizes what goes *into* the
# repository and still hands Windows CRLF on the way out, which is exactly the
# half that broke.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary, so Git never inspects or rewrites a byte. The fuzz corpus especially:
# its files are chosen byte strings, and a corpus entry Git decided to translate
# would be a different test than the one that was committed.
*.bin binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.zip binary
*.jar binary
*.keystore binary
